{"subjectId":"dsa-basics","id":"set","title":"Set","group":"Hashing","order":9,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A set stores <strong>distinct</strong> elements with no order and no index, and answers <em>is <code>x</code> present?</em> in O(1) on average. It is a hash table that stores keys only — the right structure for de-duplication, membership tests and set algebra.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Hash set</strong> — a collection of unique elements implemented with a hash table. Each element is hashed to a slot; adding an existing element does nothing, so duplicates can never appear. Python <code>set</code>, C++ <code>unordered_set</code>, Java <code>HashSet</code>, JavaScript <code>Set</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>A hash set is a hash table whose slots hold just the keys</figcaption><pre><code>add(10), add(20), add(30), add(20)  with  h(x) = x % 4\n\nslot 0 │ 20 │       ← the second add(20) finds 20 already here and does nothing\nslot 1 │    │\nslot 2 │ 10 │──▶│ 30 │\nslot 3 │    │\n\nsize = 3, iteration order follows the slots, not the insertion order</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Properties of a set</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circles\"></i>Distinct elements</h3><p>Adding an element that is already present is a no-op. <code>{1, 1, 2}</code> has size 2.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Unordered</h3><p>Elements are stored by hash slot; iteration order is not insertion order and may change after a rehash.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hash\"></i>No indexing</h3><p>There is no <code>s[0]</code> — use membership tests and iteration instead.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circles-relation\"></i>Fast set algebra</h3><p>Union, intersection and difference run in time linear in the sizes of the sets.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-bolt\"></i>Hashing internally</h3><p>Every operation is a hash lookup — O(1) average, O(n) worst case.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-lock\"></i>Hashable elements only</h3><p>Elements must be immutable / have a stable hash: numbers, strings, tuples — not Python lists or dicts.</p></div></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Python</th><th>Average</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>add</td><td><code>s.add(x)</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>Hash <code>x</code>, place it in its slot if absent</td></tr><tr><td>remove</td><td><code>s.remove(x)</code> (error if missing) / <code>s.discard(x)</code> (silent)</td><td>O(1)</td><td>Hash <code>x</code>, unlink it from its slot</td></tr><tr><td>membership</td><td><code>x in s</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>Hash <code>x</code>, compare with the keys in that slot only</td></tr><tr><td>size</td><td><code>len(s)</code></td><td>O(1)</td><td>A counter maintained on add/remove</td></tr><tr><td>union / intersection / difference</td><td><code>a | b</code>, <code>a &amp; b</code>, <code>a - b</code>, <code>a ^ b</code></td><td>O(len(a) + len(b))</td><td>Iterate one set, test membership in the other</td></tr><tr><td>subset / superset / disjoint</td><td><code>a &lt;= b</code>, <code>a &gt;= b</code>, <code>a.isdisjoint(b)</code></td><td>O(min(len(a), len(b)))</td><td>Test every element of the smaller set against the other</td></tr><tr><td>iterate</td><td><code>for x in s</code></td><td>O(n)</td><td>Walk every slot</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Set containers in the four languages</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Hash set (unordered, O(1) avg)</th><th>Ordered set (sorted, O(log n))</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Python</td><td><code>set</code>, immutable <code>frozenset</code></td><td>— (use <code>sorted(s)</code> when needed, or <code>bisect</code> on a list)</td></tr><tr><td>C++</td><td><code>std::unordered_set</code></td><td><code>std::set</code> (red-black tree)</td></tr><tr><td>Java</td><td><code>HashSet</code>; <code>LinkedHashSet</code> keeps insertion order</td><td><code>TreeSet</code> (red-black tree)</td></tr><tr><td>JavaScript</td><td><code>Set</code> (keeps insertion order by specification)</td><td>— (sort an array)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Choose the <strong>hash</strong> version by default. Switch to the <strong>ordered</strong> version only when you need sorted iteration, <code>min</code>/<code>max</code>, floor/ceiling or range queries — those cost O(log n) per operation instead of O(1).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i>Creating a set and adding elements</h3>\n<p>A set is built from a literal or from any iterable (duplicates are dropped on the way in). Elements are added one at a time or in bulk. Because iteration order differs between languages, the programs below <strong>sort</strong> before printing.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def show(label, s):\n    print(label + \":\", *sorted(s))\n\ns2 = set([1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, 4])        # duplicates dropped\nshow(\"from list with duplicates\", s2)\nprint(\"size:\", len(s2))\n\ns = {10, 20}                                # a set literal\ns.add(30)                                   # one element\nshow(\"after add 30\", s)\ns.update([40, 50])                          # any iterable\ns.update([60, 70], {80, 90})                # several at once\nshow(\"after bulk add\", s)\nprint(\"size:\", len(s))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid show(const string&amp; label, const unordered_set&lt;int&gt;&amp; s) {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; v(s.begin(), s.end());\n    sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n    cout &lt;&lt; label &lt;&lt; \":\";\n    for (int x : v) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; x;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; data = {1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, 4};\n    unordered_set&lt;int&gt; s2(data.begin(), data.end());   // duplicates dropped\n    show(\"from list with duplicates\", s2);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; s2.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n\n    unordered_set&lt;int&gt; s = {10, 20};\n    s.insert(30);                                       // one element\n    show(\"after add 30\", s);\n    s.insert({40, 50});                                 // several at once\n    for (int x : {60, 70, 80, 90}) s.insert(x);\n    show(\"after bulk add\", s);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; s.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void show(String label, Set&lt;Integer&gt; s) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(label + \":\");\n        for (int x : new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(s)) sb.append(\" \").append(x);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; s2 = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(List.of(1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, 4));   // duplicates dropped\n        show(\"from list with duplicates\", s2);\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + s2.size());\n\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; s = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(List.of(10, 20));\n        s.add(30);                                   // one element\n        show(\"after add 30\", s);\n        s.addAll(List.of(40, 50));                   // any collection\n        s.addAll(List.of(60, 70, 80, 90));\n        show(\"after bulk add\", s);\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + s.size());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const show = (label, s) =&gt; console.log(label + \":\", [...s].sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b).join(\" \"));\n\nconst s2 = new Set([1, 2, 3, 4, 1, 2, 3, 4]);   // duplicates dropped\nshow(\"from list with duplicates\", s2);\nconsole.log(\"size:\", s2.size);\n\nconst s = new Set([10, 20]);\ns.add(30);                                      // one element\nshow(\"after add 30\", s);\nfor (const x of [40, 50]) s.add(x);             // no bulk add — loop\nfor (const x of [60, 70, 80, 90]) s.add(x);\nshow(\"after bulk add\", s);\nconsole.log(\"size:\", s.size);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>from list with duplicates: 1 2 3 4\nsize: 4\nafter add 30: 10 20 30\nafter bulk add: 10 20 30 40 50 60 70 80 90\nsize: 9</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) average per add</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>In Python <code>s = {}</code> creates an empty <strong>dict</strong>, not a set — <code>type({})</code> is <code>dict</code>. The only way to write an empty set is <code>set()</code>. Set literals with elements (<code>{1, 2}</code>) are fine.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-trash\"></i>Removing elements</h3>\n<p>Python offers two ways to remove: <code>discard(x)</code> silently ignores a missing element, <code>remove(x)</code> raises <code>KeyError</code>. C++ <code>erase</code>, Java <code>remove</code> and JavaScript <code>delete</code> behave like <code>discard</code> and report whether anything was removed. <code>clear()</code> empties the set in every language.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def show(label, s):\n    print(label + \":\", *sorted(s))\n\ns = {10, 20, 30}\ns.discard(30)                               # silent if missing\nshow(\"after discard 30\", s)\nremoved = 20 in s\ns.remove(20)                                # KeyError if missing\nprint(\"removed 20:\", \"true\" if removed else \"false\")\nshow(\"after remove 20\", s)\nremoved = 99 in s\ns.discard(99)\nprint(\"removed 99:\", \"true\" if removed else \"false\")\ns.clear()\nprint(\"after clear, size:\", len(s))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nvoid show(const string&amp; label, const unordered_set&lt;int&gt;&amp; s) {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; v(s.begin(), s.end());\n    sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n    cout &lt;&lt; label &lt;&lt; \":\";\n    for (int x : v) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; x;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    unordered_set&lt;int&gt; s = {10, 20, 30};\n    s.erase(30);                                        // returns number erased (0 or 1)\n    show(\"after discard 30\", s);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"removed 20: \" &lt;&lt; (s.erase(20) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    show(\"after remove 20\", s);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"removed 99: \" &lt;&lt; (s.erase(99) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    s.clear();\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"after clear, size: \" &lt;&lt; s.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void show(String label, Set&lt;Integer&gt; s) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(label + \":\");\n        for (int x : new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(s)) sb.append(\" \").append(x);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; s = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(List.of(10, 20, 30));\n        s.remove(30);                                // returns boolean, no exception\n        show(\"after discard 30\", s);\n        System.out.println(\"removed 20: \" + s.remove(20));\n        show(\"after remove 20\", s);\n        System.out.println(\"removed 99: \" + s.remove(99));\n        s.clear();\n        System.out.println(\"after clear, size: \" + s.size());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const show = (label, s) =&gt; console.log(label + \":\", [...s].sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b).join(\" \"));\n\nconst s = new Set([10, 20, 30]);\ns.delete(30);                                   // returns boolean, no exception\nshow(\"after discard 30\", s);\nconsole.log(\"removed 20:\", s.delete(20));\nshow(\"after remove 20\", s);\nconsole.log(\"removed 99:\", s.delete(99));\ns.clear();\nconsole.log(\"after clear, size:\", s.size);</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>after discard 30: 10 20\nremoved 20: true\nafter remove 20: 10\nremoved 99: false\nafter clear, size: 0</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) average per removal</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Membership and aggregates</h3>\n<p><code>x in s</code> is the operation sets exist for: hash <code>x</code>, look in one slot. The aggregates <code>len</code>, <code>max</code>, <code>min</code>, <code>sum</code> are ordinary iterations — O(1) for <code>len</code> (a stored counter) and O(n) for the rest, because a hash set keeps no order.</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>s = {10, 20, 30, 40}\nprint(\"size:\", len(s))                     # O(1)\nprint(\"max:\", max(s))                      # O(n) — a hash set is not sorted\nprint(\"min:\", min(s))\nprint(\"sum:\", sum(s))\nprint(\"20 in s:\", \"true\" if 20 in s else \"false\")   # O(1) average\nprint(\"50 in s:\", \"true\" if 50 in s else \"false\")</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;numeric&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    unordered_set&lt;int&gt; s = {10, 20, 30, 40};\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; s.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";                       // O(1)\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"max: \" &lt;&lt; *max_element(s.begin(), s.end()) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";   // O(n) — not sorted\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"min: \" &lt;&lt; *min_element(s.begin(), s.end()) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"sum: \" &lt;&lt; accumulate(s.begin(), s.end(), 0) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"20 in s: \" &lt;&lt; (s.count(20) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";   // O(1) average\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"50 in s: \" &lt;&lt; (s.contains(50) ? \"true\" : \"false\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; // C++20\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; s = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(List.of(10, 20, 30, 40));\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + s.size());                      // O(1)\n        System.out.println(\"max: \" + Collections.max(s));             // O(n) — not sorted\n        System.out.println(\"min: \" + Collections.min(s));\n        int sum = 0;\n        for (int x : s) sum += x;\n        System.out.println(\"sum: \" + sum);\n        System.out.println(\"20 in s: \" + s.contains(20));             // O(1) average\n        System.out.println(\"50 in s: \" + s.contains(50));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const s = new Set([10, 20, 30, 40]);\nconsole.log(\"size:\", s.size);                       // O(1)\nconsole.log(\"max:\", Math.max(...s));                // O(n) — not sorted\nconsole.log(\"min:\", Math.min(...s));\nconsole.log(\"sum:\", [...s].reduce((a, b) =&gt; a + b, 0));\nconsole.log(\"20 in s:\", s.has(20));                 // O(1) average\nconsole.log(\"50 in s:\", s.has(50));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>size: 4\nmax: 40\nmin: 10\nsum: 100\n20 in s: true\n50 in s: false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) membership, O(n) aggregates</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>Replace <code>x in some_list</code> (O(n) scan) by <code>x in some_set</code> (O(1)) whenever the same collection is tested many times — the single most common hashing speed-up in interview solutions.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circles-relation\"></i>Set algebra: union, intersection, difference</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>The four set operations on two sets that share one element</figcaption><pre><code>s1 = {10, 20, 30}      s2 = {30, 40, 50}\n\nunion          s1 | s2   = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}   everything in either\nintersection   s1 &amp; s2   = {30}                   only what is in both\ndifference     s1 - s2   = {10, 20}               in s1 but not in s2\nsymmetric diff s1 ^ s2   = {10, 20, 40, 50}       in exactly one of them</code></pre></figure>\n<p>Each operation iterates one set and tests membership in the other, so it costs O(|s1| + |s2|). Python has operators and named methods (<code>s1.union(s2)</code>); C++ needs a loop (or <code>std::set_union</code> on <strong>sorted</strong> containers); Java uses the mutating <code>addAll</code> / <code>retainAll</code> / <code>removeAll</code> on a copy; JavaScript gained <code>union</code>, <code>intersection</code>, <code>difference</code> and <code>symmetricDifference</code> methods on <code>Set</code> in ES2025 (Node 22+).</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def show(label, s):\n    print(label + \":\", *sorted(s))\n\ns1 = {10, 20, 30}\ns2 = {30, 40, 50}\nshow(\"union\", s1 | s2)                      # also s1.union(s2)\nshow(\"intersection\", s1 &amp; s2)               # s1.intersection(s2)\nshow(\"difference\", s1 - s2)                 # s1.difference(s2)\nshow(\"symmetric difference\", s1 ^ s2)       # s1.symmetric_difference(s2)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;algorithm&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nusing Set = unordered_set&lt;int&gt;;\n\nvoid show(const string&amp; label, const Set&amp; s) {\n    vector&lt;int&gt; v(s.begin(), s.end());\n    sort(v.begin(), v.end());\n    cout &lt;&lt; label &lt;&lt; \":\";\n    for (int x : v) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; x;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}\n\nSet unionOf(const Set&amp; a, const Set&amp; b) { Set r = a; r.insert(b.begin(), b.end()); return r; }\nSet intersection(const Set&amp; a, const Set&amp; b) {\n    Set r;\n    for (int x : a) if (b.count(x)) r.insert(x);     // in both\n    return r;\n}\nSet difference(const Set&amp; a, const Set&amp; b) {\n    Set r;\n    for (int x : a) if (!b.count(x)) r.insert(x);    // in a, not in b\n    return r;\n}\nSet symmetricDifference(const Set&amp; a, const Set&amp; b) {\n    return unionOf(difference(a, b), difference(b, a));\n}\n\nint main() {\n    Set s1 = {10, 20, 30}, s2 = {30, 40, 50};\n    show(\"union\", unionOf(s1, s2));\n    show(\"intersection\", intersection(s1, s2));\n    show(\"difference\", difference(s1, s2));\n    show(\"symmetric difference\", symmetricDifference(s1, s2));\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static void show(String label, Set&lt;Integer&gt; s) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(label + \":\");\n        for (int x : new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(s)) sb.append(\" \").append(x);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; s1 = Set.of(10, 20, 30), s2 = Set.of(30, 40, 50);\n\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; union = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(s1); union.addAll(s2);\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; inter = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(s1); inter.retainAll(s2);      // keep only what is in s2\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; diff = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(s1); diff.removeAll(s2);        // drop what is in s2\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; sym = new HashSet&lt;&gt;(union); sym.removeAll(inter);    // union minus intersection\n\n        show(\"union\", union);\n        show(\"intersection\", inter);\n        show(\"difference\", diff);\n        show(\"symmetric difference\", sym);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const show = (label, s) =&gt; console.log(label + \":\", [...s].sort((a, b) =&gt; a - b).join(\" \"));\n\nconst s1 = new Set([10, 20, 30]);\nconst s2 = new Set([30, 40, 50]);\nshow(\"union\", s1.union(s2));                            // ES2025 Set methods\nshow(\"intersection\", s1.intersection(s2));\nshow(\"difference\", s1.difference(s2));\nshow(\"symmetric difference\", s1.symmetricDifference(s2));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>union: 10 20 30 40 50\nintersection: 30\ndifference: 10 20\nsymmetric difference: 10 20 40 50</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(|s1| + |s2|)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(|s1| + |s2|)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>The mutating forms update a set in place: Python <code>s1 |= s2</code>, <code>s1 &amp;= s2</code>, <code>s1 -= s2</code> (or <code>update</code>, <code>intersection_update</code>, <code>difference_update</code>); Java's <code>addAll</code> / <code>retainAll</code> / <code>removeAll</code> are <em>only</em> mutating, which is why the sample copies first.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-git-compare\"></i>Subset, superset and disjoint tests</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Python</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>a.isdisjoint(b)</code></td><td>No common element</td><td><code>a.isdisjoint(b)</code></td><td>O(min(|a|, |b|))</td></tr><tr><td><code>a &lt;= b</code></td><td>Every element of <code>a</code> is in <code>b</code> (subset, possibly equal)</td><td><code>a.issubset(b)</code></td><td>O(|a|)</td></tr><tr><td><code>a &lt; b</code></td><td>Subset <strong>and</strong> <code>a != b</code> (proper subset)</td><td><code>a &lt; b</code></td><td>O(|a|)</td></tr><tr><td><code>a &gt;= b</code></td><td><code>a</code> contains every element of <code>b</code> (superset)</td><td><code>a.issuperset(b)</code></td><td>O(|b|)</td></tr><tr><td><code>a &gt; b</code></td><td>Proper superset</td><td><code>a &gt; b</code></td><td>O(|b|)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def b(x): return \"true\" if x else \"false\"\n\ns1 = {10, 20}\ns2 = {40, 30, 50}\nprint(\"s1 disjoint s2:\", b(s1.isdisjoint(s2)))   # no common element\nprint(\"s2 disjoint s1:\", b(s2.isdisjoint(s1)))\nprint(\"s1 disjoint s1:\", b(s1.isdisjoint(s1)))   # a non-empty set shares everything with itself\nprint(\"s1 &lt;= s2:\", b(s1 &lt;= s2))                   # subset\nprint(\"s1 &lt; s2:\", b(s1 &lt; s2))                     # proper subset\nprint(\"s1 &gt;= s2:\", b(s1 &gt;= s2))                   # superset\nprint(\"s1 &gt; s2:\", b(s1 &gt; s2))                     # proper superset\nprint(\"{10} &lt;= s1:\", b({10} &lt;= s1))\nprint(\"{10} &lt; s1:\", b({10} &lt; s1))\nprint(\"s1 &lt;= s1:\", b(s1 &lt;= s1))                   # a set is a subset of itself …\nprint(\"s1 &lt; s1:\", b(s1 &lt; s1))                     # … but not a proper one</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\nusing namespace std;\nusing Set = unordered_set&lt;int&gt;;\n\nbool isDisjoint(const Set&amp; a, const Set&amp; b) {\n    const Set&amp; small = a.size() &lt; b.size() ? a : b;\n    const Set&amp; big = a.size() &lt; b.size() ? b : a;\n    for (int x : small) if (big.count(x)) return false;\n    return true;\n}\nbool isSubset(const Set&amp; a, const Set&amp; b) {          // a ⊆ b\n    if (a.size() &gt; b.size()) return false;\n    for (int x : a) if (!b.count(x)) return false;\n    return true;\n}\nbool isProperSubset(const Set&amp; a, const Set&amp; b) { return a.size() &lt; b.size() &amp;&amp; isSubset(a, b); }\nconst char* B(bool x) { return x ? \"true\" : \"false\"; }\n\nint main() {\n    Set s1 = {10, 20}, s2 = {40, 30, 50}, one = {10};\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s1 disjoint s2: \" &lt;&lt; B(isDisjoint(s1, s2)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s2 disjoint s1: \" &lt;&lt; B(isDisjoint(s2, s1)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s1 disjoint s1: \" &lt;&lt; B(isDisjoint(s1, s1)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s1 &lt;= s2: \" &lt;&lt; B(isSubset(s1, s2)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s1 &lt; s2: \" &lt;&lt; B(isProperSubset(s1, s2)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s1 &gt;= s2: \" &lt;&lt; B(isSubset(s2, s1)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s1 &gt; s2: \" &lt;&lt; B(isProperSubset(s2, s1)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"{10} &lt;= s1: \" &lt;&lt; B(isSubset(one, s1)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"{10} &lt; s1: \" &lt;&lt; B(isProperSubset(one, s1)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s1 &lt;= s1: \" &lt;&lt; B(isSubset(s1, s1)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"s1 &lt; s1: \" &lt;&lt; B(isProperSubset(s1, s1)) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static boolean isDisjoint(Set&lt;Integer&gt; a, Set&lt;Integer&gt; b) { return Collections.disjoint(a, b); }\n    static boolean isSubset(Set&lt;Integer&gt; a, Set&lt;Integer&gt; b) { return b.containsAll(a); }     // a ⊆ b\n    static boolean isProperSubset(Set&lt;Integer&gt; a, Set&lt;Integer&gt; b) { return a.size() &lt; b.size() &amp;&amp; b.containsAll(a); }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; s1 = Set.of(10, 20), s2 = Set.of(40, 30, 50), one = Set.of(10);\n        System.out.println(\"s1 disjoint s2: \" + isDisjoint(s1, s2));\n        System.out.println(\"s2 disjoint s1: \" + isDisjoint(s2, s1));\n        System.out.println(\"s1 disjoint s1: \" + isDisjoint(s1, s1));\n        System.out.println(\"s1 &lt;= s2: \" + isSubset(s1, s2));\n        System.out.println(\"s1 &lt; s2: \" + isProperSubset(s1, s2));\n        System.out.println(\"s1 &gt;= s2: \" + isSubset(s2, s1));\n        System.out.println(\"s1 &gt; s2: \" + isProperSubset(s2, s1));\n        System.out.println(\"{10} &lt;= s1: \" + isSubset(one, s1));\n        System.out.println(\"{10} &lt; s1: \" + isProperSubset(one, s1));\n        System.out.println(\"s1 &lt;= s1: \" + isSubset(s1, s1));\n        System.out.println(\"s1 &lt; s1: \" + isProperSubset(s1, s1));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const s1 = new Set([10, 20]);\nconst s2 = new Set([40, 30, 50]);\nconst one = new Set([10]);\nconst properSubset = (a, b) =&gt; a.size &lt; b.size &amp;&amp; a.isSubsetOf(b);\n\nconsole.log(\"s1 disjoint s2:\", s1.isDisjointFrom(s2));     // ES2025 Set methods\nconsole.log(\"s2 disjoint s1:\", s2.isDisjointFrom(s1));\nconsole.log(\"s1 disjoint s1:\", s1.isDisjointFrom(s1));\nconsole.log(\"s1 &lt;= s2:\", s1.isSubsetOf(s2));\nconsole.log(\"s1 &lt; s2:\", properSubset(s1, s2));\nconsole.log(\"s1 &gt;= s2:\", s1.isSupersetOf(s2));\nconsole.log(\"s1 &gt; s2:\", properSubset(s2, s1));\nconsole.log(\"{10} &lt;= s1:\", one.isSubsetOf(s1));\nconsole.log(\"{10} &lt; s1:\", properSubset(one, s1));\nconsole.log(\"s1 &lt;= s1:\", s1.isSubsetOf(s1));\nconsole.log(\"s1 &lt; s1:\", properSubset(s1, s1));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>s1 disjoint s2: true\ns2 disjoint s1: true\ns1 disjoint s1: false\ns1 &lt;= s2: false\ns1 &lt; s2: false\ns1 &gt;= s2: false\ns1 &gt; s2: false\n{10} &lt;= s1: true\n{10} &lt; s1: true\ns1 &lt;= s1: true\ns1 &lt; s1: false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(min(|a|, |b|))</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(1)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p><code>&lt;</code> and <code>&gt;</code> on sets are <strong>not</strong> a total order: for <code>s1 = {10, 20}</code> and <code>s2 = {30, 40, 50}</code> every one of <code>s1 &lt; s2</code>, <code>s1 &gt; s2</code> and <code>s1 == s2</code> is false. Never sort a list of sets with these operators.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-sort\"></i>Iteration order</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>A hash set iterates in <strong>slot order</strong>, which depends on the hash values and the table size — it is not insertion order, not sorted order, and it can change after a rehash. Any program whose output depends on it is a bug waiting to happen.</p></div>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Container</th><th>Iteration order</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Python <code>set</code></td><td>Arbitrary (small ints often <em>look</em> sorted because <code>hash(i) == i</code> — do not rely on it); string hashes are randomised per process</td></tr><tr><td>C++ <code>unordered_set</code></td><td>Arbitrary, implementation defined</td></tr><tr><td>Java <code>HashSet</code></td><td>Arbitrary; <code>LinkedHashSet</code> = insertion order; <code>TreeSet</code> = sorted</td></tr><tr><td>JavaScript <code>Set</code></td><td><strong>Insertion order</strong>, guaranteed by the language specification</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--good\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Do</div><p>When the output must be reproducible, sort first (<code>sorted(s)</code>), or use an insertion-ordered container (<code>LinkedHashSet</code>, JS <code>Set</code>, Python <code>dict.fromkeys(items)</code>) when the original order is what you want.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-apps\"></i>Typical uses</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy-off\"></i>De-duplication</h3><p><code>len(set(arr))</code> counts distinct values; <code>set(arr)</code> drops repeats in O(n) instead of O(n²).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-search\"></i>Fast lookups</h3><p>Turn a list of allowed values / visited states / seen ids into a set and test membership in O(1).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-route\"></i>Visited markers</h3><p>BFS/DFS on graphs store visited nodes in a set — see the Graph topics.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Pair problems</h3><p>Two-sum, pair with a given difference: scan once, test whether <code>target - x</code> has been <em>seen</em>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circles-relation\"></i>Set algebra</h3><p>Common friends = intersection; who left = difference; symmetric difference = what changed.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-chart-bar\"></i>Frequency counting</h3><p>Needs counts, not just presence — that is a dictionary (next topic).</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-copy-off\"></i>Problem: count distinct elements in an array</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [10, 20, 20, 10, 30]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>3</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>arr = [5, 5, 5]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>1</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Insert every element into a set; duplicates vanish automatically, so the set's size is the number of distinct values. One pass, O(1) average per insert.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def count_distinct(arr):\n    return len(set(arr))                  # duplicates collapse on insert\n\nfor arr in ([10, 20, 20, 10, 30], [5, 5, 5], [1, 2, 3, 4]):\n    print(*arr, \"→\", count_distinct(arr), \"distinct\")</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint countDistinct(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; arr) {\n    unordered_set&lt;int&gt; seen(arr.begin(), arr.end());   // duplicates collapse on insert\n    return (int)seen.size();\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;&gt; tests = {{10, 20, 20, 10, 30}, {5, 5, 5}, {1, 2, 3, 4}};\n    for (const auto&amp; arr : tests) {\n        for (int x : arr) cout &lt;&lt; x &lt;&lt; \" \";\n        cout &lt;&lt; \"→ \" &lt;&lt; countDistinct(arr) &lt;&lt; \" distinct\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int countDistinct(int[] arr) {\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; seen = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int x : arr) seen.add(x);              // duplicates collapse on insert\n        return seen.size();\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][] tests = {{10, 20, 20, 10, 30}, {5, 5, 5}, {1, 2, 3, 4}};\n        for (int[] arr : tests) {\n            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();\n            for (int x : arr) sb.append(x).append(\" \");\n            System.out.println(sb + \"→ \" + countDistinct(arr) + \" distinct\");\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const countDistinct = (arr) =&gt; new Set(arr).size;   // duplicates collapse on insert\n\nfor (const arr of [[10, 20, 20, 10, 30], [5, 5, 5], [1, 2, 3, 4]])\n    console.log(arr.join(\" \"), \"→\", countDistinct(arr), \"distinct\");</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10 20 20 10 30 → 3 distinct\n5 5 5 → 1 distinct\n1 2 3 4 → 4 distinct</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n) average</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-circles-relation\"></i>Problem: do two arrays have a common element?</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>a = [3, 8, 12, 5], b = [7, 1, 12]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>Yes (12)</code><br><strong>Input:</strong> <code>a = [1, 2], b = [3, 4]</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>No</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Put the smaller array in a set, then scan the other array testing membership — O(n + m) instead of the O(n · m) nested loop. This is the intersection operation stopped at the first hit.</p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>def common_element(a, b):\n    seen = set(a)                          # O(len(a))\n    for x in b:                            # O(len(b)) membership tests\n        if x in seen:\n            return x\n    return None\n\nfor a, b in (([3, 8, 12, 5], [7, 1, 12]), ([1, 2], [3, 4])):\n    found = common_element(a, b)\n    result = f\"Yes ({found})\" if found is not None else \"No\"\n    print(f\"[{' '.join(map(str, a))}] and [{' '.join(map(str, b))}] →\", result)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n#include &lt;unordered_set&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nbool commonElement(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; a, const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; b, int&amp; found) {\n    unordered_set&lt;int&gt; seen(a.begin(), a.end());   // O(len(a))\n    for (int x : b)                                // O(len(b)) membership tests\n        if (seen.count(x)) { found = x; return true; }\n    return false;\n}\n\nstring join(const vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; v) {\n    string s = \"[\";\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; (int)v.size(); i++) s += (i ? \" \" : \"\") + to_string(v[i]);\n    return s + \"]\";\n}\n\nint main() {\n    vector&lt;pair&lt;vector&lt;int&gt;, vector&lt;int&gt;&gt;&gt; tests = {{{3, 8, 12, 5}, {7, 1, 12}}, {{1, 2}, {3, 4}}};\n    for (auto&amp; [a, b] : tests) {\n        int found;\n        bool ok = commonElement(a, b, found);\n        cout &lt;&lt; join(a) &lt;&lt; \" and \" &lt;&lt; join(b) &lt;&lt; \" → \" &lt;&lt; (ok ? \"Yes (\" + to_string(found) + \")\" : \"No\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.*;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static Integer commonElement(int[] a, int[] b) {\n        Set&lt;Integer&gt; seen = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();\n        for (int x : a) seen.add(x);                   // O(len(a))\n        for (int x : b) if (seen.contains(x)) return x; // O(len(b)) membership tests\n        return null;\n    }\n\n    static String join(int[] v) {\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"[\");\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; v.length; i++) sb.append(i &gt; 0 ? \" \" : \"\").append(v[i]);\n        return sb + \"]\";\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int[][][] tests = {{{3, 8, 12, 5}, {7, 1, 12}}, {{1, 2}, {3, 4}}};\n        for (int[][] t : tests) {\n            Integer found = commonElement(t[0], t[1]);\n            System.out.println(join(t[0]) + \" and \" + join(t[1]) + \" → \" + (found != null ? \"Yes (\" + found + \")\" : \"No\"));\n        }\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function commonElement(a, b) {\n    const seen = new Set(a);                 // O(len(a))\n    for (const x of b)                       // O(len(b)) membership tests\n        if (seen.has(x)) return x;\n    return null;\n}\n\nconst join = (v) =&gt; \"[\" + v.join(\" \") + \"]\";\nfor (const [a, b] of [[[3, 8, 12, 5], [7, 1, 12]], [[1, 2], [3, 4]]]) {\n    const found = commonElement(a, b);\n    console.log(join(a), \"and\", join(b), \"→\", found !== null ? `Yes (${found})` : \"No\");\n}</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[3 8 12 5] and [7 1 12] → Yes (12)\n[1 2] and [3 4] → No</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(n + m) average</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(min(n, m))</span></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How is a set different from a list / array?</summary><div><p>A set holds each value at most once, has no positions (no <code>s[i]</code>, no order), and tests membership in O(1) average via hashing. A list keeps duplicates and order, supports O(1) indexing, but membership is an O(n) scan. Use a list when order or duplicates matter, a set when you ask <em>is it there?</em> repeatedly.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why must set elements be hashable / immutable?</summary><div><p>The element's hash decides which slot it lives in. If the element could change after insertion, its hash would change and it would be in the wrong slot — lookups would miss it. Python therefore refuses <code>list</code> and <code>dict</code> elements (<code>TypeError: unhashable type</code>) and offers <code>tuple</code> and <code>frozenset</code>; Java and C++ let you insert mutable objects but <em>break</em> if you mutate them inside the set.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What are the complexities of union, intersection and difference?</summary><div><p>All three iterate one set and test membership in the other: O(|a| + |b|) for union and symmetric difference, O(min(|a|, |b|)) for intersection if you iterate the smaller one, O(|a|) for <code>a - b</code>. With sorted (tree) sets the same operations are O(|a| + |b|) by merging.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>When would you pick a TreeSet / std::set over a hash set?</summary><div><p>When you need the elements sorted, the smallest/largest element, the nearest value above or below a given one (<code>ceiling</code>/<code>floor</code>, <code>lower_bound</code>), or a range of elements. A hash set cannot answer any of those without a full scan; a tree set does each in O(log n).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you remove duplicates from a list while keeping the original order?</summary><div><p>A set alone loses order. Keep a <code>seen</code> set and append to a result only when the element was not yet seen — O(n). In Python <code>list(dict.fromkeys(items))</code> does the same in one line because dicts preserve insertion order; in Java use <code>LinkedHashSet</code>; JavaScript's <code>[...new Set(items)]</code> works because <code>Set</code> keeps insertion order.</p></div></details></section>"}